00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.030
Mind your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:05.730
π,π,π,π.

00:00:06.279 --> 00:00:10.829
Write an equation that uses π exactly 4 times and equals 0.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:16.618
Do the same thing for 1 and 2, and see how high you can go.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.029
For a real challenge, solve for any integer n.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.379
You can use common mathematical operations

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.049
limited to the following table.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.730
These are standard functions you would find on an Android calculator.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.040
I thank many people who suggested similar problems,

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.880
including Tai C. Chiang, Peter,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.080
and the Puzzling StackExchange community.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:49.860
Pause the video if you'd like to give this problem a try, and when you're ready, keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.600
We'll get started with an equation for 0.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:10.120
Here's one of the many equations you could've written.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.500
π + π - π - π=0.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.660
From here, rather than writing formulas for specific numbers,

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.340
let's write a general formula for any positive integer n.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.320
To do that, we'll solve a similar problem.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:30.399
How can you make n from 3 copies of 2?

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.740
There's a clever solution from the Nobel laureate physicist Paul Dirac.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:38.740
Here was his formula.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.729
Let's write the following:

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:44.780
-log base 2 of log base 2.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.400
Now we are going to iterate the square root function a total of n times.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:58.949
In the end ,we're going to evaluate √2. This exactly equals n. Why is that?

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:03.900
Let's understand the formula by working through this nested square root.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:09.119
Recall that √2 = 2^(1/2).

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:16.678
Therefore,  √(√2) = 2^(1/2)^2.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.429
If we iterate the square root function a total of n times,

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.410
we'll end up with 2^(1/(2^n)).

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:29.609
This is also written as 2^(2^-n).

00:02:30.459 --> 00:02:36.509
We now evaluate log base 2 of this, which will be equal to 2^-n.

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:44.000
Evaluating the log base 2 again, we get -(-n), and the opposite of -n is n;

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.260
therefore, this formula is exactly true.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:49.940
Now, let's solve our problem with π.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.570
One thing we can do is we can change the base of this logarithm and the

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.600
argument in this nested square root to both be π.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:00.630
Both of these will cancel out.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:06.879
We can then rewrite 2 as equaling (- cos π) - cos π.

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.699
We thus have a valid expression that uses π a total of 4 times.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.300
This will give all positive integers n.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.920
We can change the negative sign into a positive to get all negative values of n.

00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.300
But we have one problem:

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.849
the logarithm function is using a base of 2.

00:03:28.849 --> 00:03:32.828
Log base 2 doesn't appear on a standard Android calculator.

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.390
So, how do we get around this?

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.559
We'll use the change of base formula for logarithms

00:03:39.019 --> 00:03:44.529
log base 2 of x = ln x/ln 2.

00:03:45.590 --> 00:03:48.009
We'll use this formula ,and substitute n.

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.039
We end up with the following fraction.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.169
Now here we have log base π, so we'll again use the change of base formula.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.079
So now let's substitute that in, and put everything in terms of the natural logarithm.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:10.200
Now, we have a negative sign at the beginning, which we can cleverly eliminate by taking the reciprocal of this argument.

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.680
This will make the formula a little bit easier to read.

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.880
We now have a formula for all positive integers n.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.360
Then, if we want to get all negative integers, we simply have a negative sign.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:29.360
You can also see that if n = 0, this entire thing will actually evaluate to be 0 as well.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.480
So we have one simple formula, and it uses π exactly four times.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:35.860
Incredible!

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:45.240
Thanks for making Mind your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching, and thanks for your support.

